cartridge "GUESS AMERICA!/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1984"

' GUESS AMERICA!
' A ColecoVision game based on "Guess America!", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' March 1984 (Commodore 64, BASIC; no author named in the program, which
' was retyped for the disk by a reader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (orig/guessam.bas): a Jumble of American history. Five words a
' game, each picked at random from the DATA (33 words, three clues each)
' and shown with its letters scrambled (lines 5000-5190: a shuffle, redone
' up to five times while it spells the word). You have a time limit to
' unscramble it. Right with no clue: 10 points. Wrong or out of time: a
' clue is shown and you try again for 7, then 5 after the second clue, 2
' after the third; after that the word is shown, no points (lines
' 6060-6290). The total (at most 50) decides how far west your wagon rolls:
' under 20 STUCK ON EAST COAST, 20-29 ALMOST TO THE MISSISSIPPI, 30-39 MADE
' IT TO THE ROCKIES, 40 and over CALIFORNIA AND GOLD! (lines 9000-9850).
'
' Liberties:
'  - Answers are built from the scrambled letters with the stick (left and
'    right choose a letter block, the left button lays it in the next
'    answer slot, the right button takes the last one back) instead of
'    typed; the word is judged when every letter is laid. The time limit is
'    30 seconds a try instead of the listing's 15 (a count of 1000 GET
'    loops), because picking with a stick is slower than typing.
'  - The DATA's heading record (GUESS / WORD,CLUE,CLUE CLUE), which the
'    listing could pick as a puzzle, is left out; a word is not asked twice
'    in one game (the listing could repeat one).
'  - The wagon is shown on the map after every word at the stop the score
'    so far reaches (the listing showed it only at the end), with the
'    stops marked under the map. The map is drawn in tiles by region with
'    the Mississippi and the Rockies (the C64 drew an outline of Z's).
'  - The final score is shown, not flashed five times; no play-again
'    question (the title follows).
'
' Controls: left/right choose a letter, left button lays it down, right
' button takes the last one back. Menus: up/down and a button.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29; tiles from code 128; screens drawn
' with the NMI off; music first in the sound table; the title tune plays
' once; nothing flashes.

